\title{Inductive Types}
\date{2024-01-30}
\taxon{Type Theory}
\import{macros}
\p{
    In the previous note, we saw the definition of natural numbers as an inductive type.
    Here we study more examples, including the unit type, empty type, coproducts,
    integers, and dependent pairs.
    Refer to [Egbert Rijke's Book](hott-2022).
}
\p{
    This section is much more informal than the previous one.
    Without displaying the inference rules, we will just present the \strong{constructor}
    and \strong{induction principle} of each inductive type.
}
\block{\strong{General Inductive Type}}{
    \p{
    Just like #{\N}, other inductive types can be defined by
    their constructors and induction principles (and computation rules).
    }
    \ul{
        \li{
            The constructors specify how canonical terms of the type are formed.
        }
        \li{
            The induction principle specifies the data required to construct 
                a section of an arbitrary type family over the inductive type.
        }
        \li{
            The computation rules specify the behavior of the constructors.
        }
    }
}
\transclude{def-0020}
\p{
    A degenerate inductive type is the \strong{empty type}.
    It has no constructor, and its induction principle is trivial.
    The empty type corresponds to the \strong{ex falso quodlibet} principle in logic.
}
\transclude{def-0021}
\p{
    With the empty type we can define the \strong{negation} of a type
    and the evidence for a negation.
}
\transclude{def-0022}
\transclude{def-0023}
\block{\strong{Special Case of Coproduct}}{
    The special case of coproduct type is also called the \strong{sum type}.
    ##{
        \ind{+}: (A \to X) \to (B \to X) \to (A+B \to X)
    }
    which is very similar to the \strong{elimination rule} of disjunction in logic.
    ##{
        (P\to Q) \to (R\to Q) \to (P\vee R\to Q)
    }
}
\subtree{
    \title{The integers}
    \p{
        Before quotient types are available, the integers can be built directly from coproducts:
        ##{
            \mathbb{Z} \defeq \mathbb{N} + (\mathbf{1}+\mathbb{N}).
        }
        The left summand represents the negative integers, while the two pieces of the right
        summand represent zero and the positive integers. In particular,
        ##{
            -1 \defeq \mathsf{inl}(0),\qquad
            0 \defeq \mathsf{inr}(\mathsf{inl}(\star)),\qquad
            1 \defeq \mathsf{inr}(\mathsf{inr}(0)).
        }
        Thus every integer has a unique canonical form: #{-(n+1)}, #{0}, or #{n+1}.
    }
    \p{
        Coproduct and natural-number induction give an induction principle for #{\mathbb{Z}}.
        It is enough to provide a value at #{0}, a value at #{1}, a value at #{-1}, and compatible
        successor steps in the positive and negative directions. This principle defines the
        successor and predecessor maps without first constructing a quotient of
        #{\mathbb{N}\times\mathbb{N}}.
    }
}
\p{
    The dependent version of a product is traditionally called the
    \strong{dependent sum type} or \strong{dependent pair type}.
    Its terms are ordered pairs.
}
\transclude{def-0024}
